From 5c5b56e86af0b4118f2e912fb3f11edf05c530e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:11:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 20/20] Keep translations a write fault invalidates and put
 them back when the same code returns

Copy protections decrypt a function, run it and encrypt it again, and FEX translated the function at every round:
Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024's main thread rewrote translated instruction bytes about 380 times a second on an M4,
and its stutter followed that thread's translation time (2026-10-18).

A block compiled normally (no single step, breakpoints or instruction limit, every instruction decoded) records an
XXH3 hash of its guest instruction bytes. A write fault on code retires the page's blocks into a preallocated 4-way
table of their code buffer's map (16,384 entries, about 2 MB a buffer; nothing is allocated in the fault path)
instead of dropping them. On a lookup miss CompileBlock looks there first: a retired version whose pages are still
executable has its pages protected again, then its bytes are hashed, and when they match, its host code is mapped
back without translating (code buffers only grow, and the table goes with its buffer). Any other invalidation
(protection change, free, unmap, an untracked write) purges retired blocks on its pages, so a block whose pages'
executability changed never comes back, and a cache clear empties the table. An instruction that failed to decode
now counts as instruction bytes for the store replay (15 bytes from its address), since it is compiled as a fault
decided by those bytes. HB_FEX_SMC_RETIRE=0 turns retirement off. ThreadStats' last two slots count reinstated
blocks and retired versions found with other bytes.

smcretire.exe on an M4: decrypt, run, encrypt 41 against 155 us a round, two versions in turn 38 against 171 us,
beside a thread writing data on the same page 3.4 times faster, eight versions in turn and a protection change
between rounds right; 0 stale calls in every case. smcdata.exe, smcpatch.exe and smcpatch32.exe, the fiber, memory,
context, debug-register (64 and 32-bit), string-resume and timer tests pass.
